    "result_text": "The build is green. Here's what I changed to satisfy the spec with minimal structural impact:\n\n## Summary\n\nThe old `membershipTier` was a **derived** field \u2014 computed at mapping time from the persisted `email` and `namesakeCount`. The new `membershipLevel` derives from the *same two inputs*, so I kept it derived (no new entity field / DB column / migration needed) and touched only three files:\n\n1. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 replaced the `membershipTier` (string) response property with `membershipLevel` (integer 1\u20133). This regenerates `OwnerDto` with `get/setMembershipLevel` and drops `membershipTier`.\n\n2. **`OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 swapped the single `@Mapping` expression to compute the level: `min(3, 1 + (email present ? 1 : 0) + (namesakeCount == 0 ? 1 : 0))`. Same base=1, +email, +namesake-zero, capped at 3, leaving 4 free for tenure.\n\n3. **`RespondWithOwner.java`** \u2014 the household loop existed only to set `membershipTier = \"GOLD\"`, which no longer exists (and the spec's level scheme has no household rule). Removing it also drops the now-unused `OwnerRepository` dependency, collapsing the method to a one-liner map-and-send.\n\nOn the audit line: the spec says \"*anything that recorded the tier ... must record the level instead*\", but the actual `AuditOwnerCreated` records only `id`, `customerCode`, and `registrationDate` \u2014 it never recorded the tier, so there was nothing to change there.\n\nTests: `AcceptanceTest.coreNumericLevel` (asserts `membershipLevel == 3` for an owner with email and no namesakes) and the full suite pass \u2014 **BUILD SUCCESS**.",
